### [05:28:16 UTC] Step 1 — Observe: read rules, README, factblock, source
Input: Task from iterate-fat-llama coordinator (cycle 1, branch iterate-fat-llama/20260906-044742, issue #20). Directive: upscale() with target_bitrate_kbps=900, adaptive_filter disabled -> unrealistic bitrate ~5300kbps/sample rate ~250kHz; adaptive_filter enabled -> 30+ min runtime hang. Two candidate root causes named: (1) upscale_factor = round(target_bitrate_kbps*1000/source_bitrate) formula conflates compressed-source-bitrate with uncompressed-target scale; (2) lms_filter is a sequential per-sample Python loop, worse with inflated sample count.
Action: Read .claude/agents/rules/scientific-coding.md, .claude/agents/rules/code-quality.md, README.md in full, docs/CURRENT_STATE.md (factblock), fat_llama/audio_fattener/feed.py in full, fat_llama/tests/test_feed.py in full. Verified cupy device ops fail in this sandbox (cudaErrorInsufficientDriver, no GPU) via a quick `cp.array([1,2,3])` probe -- confirms the prior cycle's report that only non-cupy tests (test_read_audio, test_write_audio) can execute locally.
Reasoning: Need ground truth on exact formula/implementation before hypothesizing a fix; factblock is detailed and largely current (matches source read) except it will go stale once feed.py changes below land.
Output: Confirmed feed.py:536-538 computes `upscale_factor = round(target_bitrate_kbps*1000/bitrate)` with bitrate = source's own *compressed* bitrate from mutagen (e.g. mp3 ~128-192kbps), against target_bitrate_kbps in [800,1411] (flac)/[800,6444] (wav) -- ratio routinely lands at 5-7+ for realistic inputs, confirming root cause 1. lms_filter (feed.py:263-355) is a strict Python for-loop over every output sample with several small cupy kernel calls per iteration (slice, dot, subtract, elementwise update, clip, store) -- confirming root cause 2, compounded by root cause 1's inflated sample count. docs/CURRENT_STATE.md's descriptions of both functions matched the source read (not stale as of this read, but will be stale after this cycle's edit -- noting in factblock_stale in final report). Existing test test_target_bitrate_kbps_drives_upscale_factor_not_output_bitrate (GPU-gated) explicitly asserts the *current buggy* formula/divergence as "intended, not a defect" -- this needs revision as part of the fix, not left as regression coverage for a behavior we are removing.
### [05:32:00 UTC] Step 2 — Hypothesize
Input: Findings from Step 1.
Action: Formed two hypotheses, one per root cause.
Reasoning:
  H1 (sample rate/bitrate): upscale_factor's formula divides a target value calibrated to the *compressed-file* bitrate range against the source's own compressed bitrate, with no ceiling -- replacing it with a formula that (a) still uses target_bitrate_kbps as the numerator so its documented "higher target -> larger factor" contract is preserved, but (b) clamps the result so sample_rate * upscale_factor never exceeds a realistic consumer playback ceiling (192 kHz, matching this cycle's own test framing) and never drops below 1, will produce realistic sample rates/effective bitrates for the same inputs that previously produced 250-300+ kHz outputs.
  H2 (runtime): lms_filter's slowness is Python-loop/kernel-launch overhead (many small sequential cupy calls per output sample), not raw compute -- replacing the per-sample update with a block-adaptive LMS variant (fixed weights within a block, vectorized FIR output + block-averaged gradient update once per block, reducing to the exact per-sample math at block_size=1) will cut the number of sequential Python/GPU-launch iterations by ~block_size while remaining a standard, documented LMS variant (still sequential/adaptive across blocks, not a different algorithm class, no AI/ML mechanism).
Prediction: H1 -- a new pure-Python `compute_upscale_factor()` helper (no cupy dependency) exercised with the issue's own reported inputs (typical mp3 bitrate, target_bitrate_kbps=900 and 1400) should return a factor such that sample_rate*factor <= 192000, in contrast to the old formula which returns >=5-7 for those inputs. This is directly executable and verifiable in this sandbox (no GPU needed). H2 -- cannot be executed/timed in this sandbox (no GPU); verification is limited to (a) an algebraic proof that block_size=1 reduces exactly to the prior per-sample update rule (documented in code comments), and (b) a pure-Python unit test of the new block-partitioning helper's coverage/no-overlap property extracted from the loop, plus keeping/relying on the existing @requires_gpu regression tests for the actual filtering behavior on a real GPU. This is an environmental limitation, not a gap in reasoning.
Output: (see Step 3 experiment logs below)
### [05:40:00 UTC] Step 3 — Experiment/Analyze H1 (upscale_factor)
Input: New compute_upscale_factor() added to feed.py; upscale() updated to call it.
Action: Ran `python3 -c "from fat_llama.audio_fattener.feed import compute_upscale_factor..."` against the issue's own reported cases: (44100, 192000bps, target=1400kbps) and (44100, 128000bps, target=900kbps), plus an unknown-bitrate fallback and an already-high-bitrate source.
Reasoning: This is pure Python (no cupy device call), so it is directly executable in this GPU-less sandbox -- a real, not just reasoned-about, verification of H1.
Output: factor=4 (was 7) for both reported cases -> new_sr=176400 Hz (was 308700 Hz), within the 192kHz realistic ceiling in every case; already-high-bitrate source correctly clamps to factor=1 (no downscale). Matches prediction exactly. H1 CONFIRMED.
### [05:55:00 UTC] Step 4 — Implement + regression tests
Input: H1 confirmed (Step 3). H2 needs a CUDA-appropriate implementation that is at least partially testable without a GPU.
Action: Implemented compute_upscale_factor() (new, pure-Python helper) and wired it into upscale() replacing the old inline unbounded ratio. Implemented lms_filter() as block-adaptive LMS (Clark et al. 1981 Block LMS variant): tap weights held fixed within each block of block_size (default 256) samples, block's filter output computed via a num_taps-length loop of vectorized elementwise CuPy ops over the whole block, weights updated once per block using the block-mean instantaneous gradient (algebraically identical to the prior per-sample update at block_size=1). Extracted the block-partitioning loop into a standalone pure-Python generator _lms_block_ranges() specifically so it could be unit-tested without a GPU. Added two new non-gated tests: test_compute_upscale_factor_bounds_realistic_sample_rate (exercises the issue's own reported (source_bitrate, target_bitrate_kbps) pairs plus edge cases: unknown bitrate, already-high-bitrate source, already-high sample rate) and test_lms_block_ranges_partitions_range_exactly (verifies the block partition covers [start,n) with no gaps/overlaps for several (start,n,block_size) combinations). Ran both via `python3 -m unittest` (pytest not installed in this sandbox) since they require no cupy device ops.
Reasoning: compute_upscale_factor and _lms_block_ranges were deliberately factored out as pure-Python so this cycle's fix could be genuinely, locally verified rather than only reasoned about, given the sandbox has no functional GPU (cudaErrorInsufficientDriver). The full lms_filter() filtering math (and upscale() end-to-end) still requires cupy device execution and cannot be run/timed here -- an environmental limitation, not a gap in the fix's design; block_size=1 exactness was verified algebraically (documented in lms_filter's own docstring) since it can't be executed here.
Output: Both new tests pass locally: `python3 -m unittest ... -v` -> "Ran 2 tests in 0.184s / OK". H1 fully verified end-to-end (formula + bound). H2's structural/partitioning correctness verified locally; the actual runtime speedup and per-block filtering numerics remain unverified in this sandbox (no GPU) -- flagged for the next audio-quality-checker/code-tester-reviewer GPU pass.
